//! Assertions for tests: `assert`, `assert_eq` and `assert_ne`.
//!
//! Each checks a condition and, if it does not hold, panics with a message
//! that starts `assertion failed:`, which fails the test that called it. A
//! test marked `@test(should_panic)` passes only if it panics, which is the
//! way to check that something panics.
//!
//! These functions exist only when `nova test` compiles the program. There,
//! every module sees them without an import, and a module's own definition
//! of one of them shadows the one here. `nova run`, `nova build` and
//! `nova check` leave out both `@test` functions and this module, so call
//! these functions only from `@test` functions: called anywhere else, they
//! make those commands fail, reporting a function they cannot find.

// std/test — assertions for `@test` functions.
//
// Seeded ONLY when compiling under `nova test` (see `STD_TEST_MODULE`), not
// into every program. A top-level `pub fn assert` in an always-embedded module
// would be glob-imported and take the names `assert`, `assert_eq` and
// `assert_ne` in every module of every program — the same hazard that made
// `join` hang off the separator (`",".join(parts)`) rather than being a free
// function. The cost is that `assert` is unavailable in ordinary code; `panic`
// already serves that need.
//
// Failure is `panic`, which aborts with exit non-zero and `nova: panic: ...` on
// stderr. That is the "panicked" outcome `nova test` looks for, and it is why
// there is no `assert_throws` here: catching a panic needs unwinding, and
// `nova_rt_panic_str` calls `std::process::abort()`. `@test(should_panic)` is
// the supported way to assert that something panics.

/// Checks that `cond` is `true`.
///
/// Panics if it is `false`, with the message `assertion failed: ` followed by
/// `msg`.
pub fn assert(cond: Bool, msg: String) { if !cond { panic("assertion failed: ${msg}") } }

/// Checks that `a` and `b` are equal.
///
/// Panics if `a.ne(b)` is `true`, with a message that shows both values in
/// their `Debug` form either side of `!=`, such as
/// `assertion failed: "a" != "b"`.
pub fn assert_eq<T: Eq + Debug>(a: T, b: T) {
    if a.ne(b) { panic("assertion failed: ${a.dbg()} != ${b.dbg()}") }
}

/// Checks that `a` and `b` are not equal.
///
/// Panics if `a.eq(b)` is `true`, with a message that shows both values in
/// their `Debug` form either side of `==`, such as `assertion failed: 1 == 1`.
pub fn assert_ne<T: Eq + Debug>(a: T, b: T) {
    if a.eq(b) { panic("assertion failed: ${a.dbg()} == ${b.dbg()}") }
}
